WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.119
Querying is where a hunt hypothesis becomes an evidence search.

2
00:00:04.119 --> 00:00:11.594
The exact syntax depends on the SIEM, data lake, EDR console, or log platform, but the thinking is provider-neutral.

3
00:00:11.594 --> 00:00:23.417
Start with the behavior, identify the required fields, select the data source, define the time window, and write a query or filter that returns enough context to reason about the event.

4
00:00:23.417 --> 00:00:29.290
A good query is not just a string that produces results. It is a documented test of a hunt idea.

5
00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:33.333
Pivots help the analyst move from one clue to related evidence.

6
00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:39.283
A host-to-user pivot asks which identities were active on a system around a time of interest.

7
00:00:39.283 --> 00:00:46.071
A user-to-source-IP pivot asks where an account authenticated from and whether that location is normal.

8
00:00:46.071 --> 00:00:55.987
A process-to-parent-process pivot asks what launched a suspicious command. A domain-to-DNS-clients pivot asks which systems resolved a destination.

9
00:00:55.987 --> 00:01:04.225
A hash-to-endpoints pivot asks where a file appeared. An alert-to-timeline pivot asks what happened before and after a signal.

10
00:01:04.225 --> 00:01:11.319
Each pivot should have a purpose tied to the hypothesis. Noise reduction is necessary, but it can be dangerous.

11
00:01:11.319 --> 00:01:23.676
Large environments produce routine administrative tools, software updates, cloud automation, service accounts, vulnerability scanners, and user behavior that may look odd in isolation.

12
00:01:23.676 --> 00:01:29.092
Filters help remove known benign patterns, but over-filtering can hide the signal.

13
00:01:29.092 --> 00:01:35.270
If you exclude all administrative accounts, you may miss compromised administrator activity.

14
00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:39.694
If you exclude all internal tools, you may miss misuse of those tools.

15
00:01:39.694 --> 00:01:48.237
A better approach is to filter deliberately and document why each exclusion is reasonable. Baselines are useful for reducing noise.

16
00:01:48.237 --> 00:01:57.238
Instead of asking whether a process ever runs, ask whether it runs on this host type, under this parent, by this user, during this time window.

17
00:01:57.238 --> 00:02:04.637
Instead of asking whether a domain is rare globally, ask whether it is rare for this business unit or endpoint group.

18
00:02:04.637 --> 00:02:11.731
Baselines are not perfect proof, but they help separate ordinary from unusual. Save queries and assumptions.

19
00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:14.782
A hunt should be repeatable by another analyst.

20
00:02:14.782 --> 00:02:21.799
Save the query text, data source, time window, filters, field names, known exclusions, and any limitations.

21
00:02:21.799 --> 00:02:28.130
If a field name changes or a parser breaks, future reviewers need to know what the query depended on.

22
00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:33.241
The beginner mistake is to treat querying as the hunt itself. Querying is a tool.

23
00:02:33.241 --> 00:02:44.454
The hunt is the structured process of asking a question, collecting evidence, pivoting carefully, reducing noise responsibly, and documenting what the evidence supports.

24
00:02:44.454 --> 00:02:48.801
Provider-neutral thinking helps when teams use more than one tool.

25
00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:57.116
One SIEM may call a field process_name, another may call it image, and an EDR console may show the same concept in a different panel.

26
00:02:57.116 --> 00:03:06.650
The hunter should understand the concept behind the field. That understanding makes the hunt portable and reduces dependence on memorized syntax.
